#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049195 :topic/id #uuid "e278bb36-cd42-5d79-a581-bd3a23f09b21" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054622 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054537 :question/id #uuid "5a566673-4529-57b8-87c9-d5a6118e0398" :question/math-academy-id "e-4082" :question/problem "Express $\\log(3)+4\\log(y)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite the second term to obtain\n\n$$\n\\log(3)+4\\log(y)=\\log(3)+\\log(y^4).\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the two terms to get\n\n$$\n\\log(3)+\\log(y^4)=\\log\\left(3y^4\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log(3)+4\\log(y)=\\log\\left(3y^4\\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4dc94edb-007d-5fe5-b9ca-9ed754adc457" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting an Expression Using the Product and Power Rules of Logarithms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054623 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054538 :question/id #uuid "b0e6a6fc-18fa-57d8-9b4a-2fbd1d7fbb9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4083" :question/problem "Express $3\\log(x) - 2\\log(5)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite both terms of the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{log}⁡(5) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{3})-\\operatorname{log}⁡({5}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{3})-\\operatorname{log}⁡(25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quotient rule for logarithms, we can combine the two terms to get\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(x^3\\right) - \\log(25) =\\log\\left( \\dfrac{x^3}{25} \\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log(x) - 2\\log(5) =  \\log\\left( \\dfrac{x^3}{25} \\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2c2df20-2d53-5c2e-a633-9442e118a6b8" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting an Expression Using the Quotient and Power Rules of Logarithms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054624 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054539 :question/id #uuid "c6e57845-614f-5be1-8551-d85d4ffc490e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4085" :question/problem "Express $\\ln(2)+\\ln(x)-\\ln(y)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the product rule for natural logarithms, we can combine the first two terms of the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quotient rule for natural logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\ln(2x)-\\ln(y) = \\ln \\left( \\dfrac{2x}{y} \\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\ln(2)+\\ln(x)-\\ln(y) = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{2x}{y}\\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22382eaf-2654-5c65-b7ba-ac71015e84b8" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting an Expression Using the Product and Quotient Rules of Logarithms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054625 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054542 :question/id #uuid "de20802c-6a7e-5d77-b2ce-26f98dc68b4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4095" :question/problem "Express $3\\log(2)+\\log(x)-\\log(4)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite the first term of the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{3})+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the first two terms to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(8x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(8x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{8x}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log(2)+\\log(x)-\\log(4) = \\log\\left(2x\\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dd9426d-8ce3-564d-85c5-4ae82289325d" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting an Expression Using the Product, Quotient, and Power Rules of Logarithms"}] :topic/math-academy-id 30 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049443}] :topic/title "Combining the Laws of Logarithms"}]}
